---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/music/intermediate/harmony-and-chords">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an intermediate lesson in Music. It assumes "Sound and Music" (frequency,
    overtones, and why simple ratios matter) and "Scales &amp; Keys" (the major scale and
    its degrees), because harmony is built by stacking thirds on scale degrees.
    Everything else (intervals, chords, cadences) is constructed here.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>name the landmark intervals and say why simple frequency ratios sound consonant;</li>
    <li>build the four triad qualities and the seven chords of a major key, and state the primary chords' functions;</li>
    <li>explain what the dominant seventh adds and why V7 to I is the strongest resolution;</li>
    <li>recognise the core progressions (I-IV-V-I, ii-V-I, the Axis progression, the 12-bar blues) and the four cadences by label and by ear;</li>
    <li>describe how the same tension-and-release logic works in traditions that refuse chords altogether.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Harmony is <strong>notes sounding together, organised into journeys</strong>. Its
    physics: simple frequency ratios sound stable (consonance), complex ones create pull
    (dissonance). Its craft: stacking thirds into chords, chaining chords into
    progressions that create and discharge tension, and cadencing home. This page builds
    the machine — intervals, triads, the primary and secondary chords, the dominant
    seventh, the four cadences, pedals — and then shows the same machine running in
    blues, jazz, pop, film scoring and in traditions that refuse chords altogether. The
    single idea to keep: <strong>harmony is prediction management</strong> — chords teach
    the ear to want things, then decide when it gets them.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the consonance–tension–resolution machine" widget="equationStep" config={harmCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> read the panels as one machine assembled in order:
    two-note flavours first, then three-note settlements, then journeys between the
    settlements, then the drama of delaying the release. Keep asking the question the
    whole page answers: what does each chord make the ear expect, and when does the music
    pay up?
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> harmony is prediction management: consonance settles,
    dissonance creates wanting, and thirds stacked into chords give the wants and the
    settlements names. Now build the working set of chords a key provides.
  </p>

  <h2>Intervals: the atoms of agreement</h2>
  <p>
    Play two notes together and their frequencies either mesh or grate. An octave (2:1)
    and a perfect fifth (3:2) overlap cleanly in the inner ear and sound <em>consonant</em>;
    a semitone cluster's complicated ratio produces <strong>beating</strong> — a physical
    roughness the ear hears as <em>dissonance</em>. Name the landmarks in semitones:
    minor third (3), major third (4), perfect fourth (5), tritone (6 — exactly half an
    octave, once nicknamed "the devil in music"), perfect fifth (7), octave (12).
    Notice the framing: dissonance is not
    "bad sound" but <em>energy</em> — an unstable interval that demands movement, which is
    exactly what makes it useful. Western music's whole harmonic architecture is built
    from one move: choosing notes a <strong>third</strong> apart (four semitones = major
    third, three = minor third) and stacking them.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="pair each landmark interval with its size" widget="match" config={hcIntervalMatch} />

  <h2>Triads and the chords of a key</h2>
  <p>
    Stack two thirds and you have a <strong>triad</strong>. Four qualities matter: major
    (bright, settled — C-E-G), minor (darker, equally settled — C-E♭-G), diminished
    (two small thirds: tense, unstable, dramatic), and augmented (two large thirds:
    weightless, floating). Build a triad on each degree of the major scale and you get
    the key's working set: <strong>I</strong> major, <strong>ii</strong> minor,
    <strong>iii</strong> minor, <strong>IV</strong> major, <strong>V</strong> major,
    <strong>vi</strong> minor, <strong>vii°</strong> diminished. The <strong>primary
    chords</strong> are I, IV and V — home, departure, tension — and they alone
    harmonise thousands of folk songs (blues uses them exclusively, as we will see).
    The
    <strong>secondary chords</strong> (ii, iii, vi, vii°) add shading: vi is the
    "melancholy substitute" for I, ii is the elegant approach to V, vii° is V's
    compressed understudy. Reading a chord chart is reading these functions, not just
    letter names. And the stacking rule explains the colours for free: a triad's quality
    depends only on the size of its two thirds, so the entire palette of Western chord
    feelings — bright, dark, tense, floating — is three sizes of third chosen twice.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="file the triad colours" widget="sort-buckets" config={hcTriadBuckets} />
  <p>
    A worked harmonisation makes the system audible. "Twinkle, Twinkle" in C major runs
    C-C-G-G-A-A-G, F-F-E-E-D-D-C — eight two-note groups. Try the primary chords only:
    C-C sit on I; G-G on V; A-A on IV (A is a third above IV's root F); the lone G on I
    (it is I's fifth) or V; F-F on IV; E-E on I; D-D on V; final C on I. Eight groups,
    three chords, and every note is accounted for — the same analysis children do at
    the piano by trial, and Bach did by craft. Note also how chords are spelled in the
    wild: pop charts use letter symbols with qualities (C, G7, Am, F♯m7♭5), theory
    uses Roman numerals precisely because they are <em>key-independent</em> — the same
    progression relocates to any key by renaming its numerals.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "Roman numerals are just fancy letter names." A letter
  name (C major) is tied to one key; a numeral (I) names a <em>function</em> that
  relocates to any key unchanged, which is the whole reason theory charts use them. The
  same I-V-vi-IV progression plays in C, in G, or in any other key by renaming the
  numerals alone.</p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="find the faulty line — the numeral confusion" widget="spot-error" config={hcNumeralSpot} />

  <h2>The dominant seventh: the engine</h2>
  <p>
    Add a minor third on top of a V triad (G-B-D plus F) and you create a
    <strong>dominant seventh</strong> — and a <strong>tritone</strong> between its third
    and seventh, the most unstable interval in the system. The tritone pulls inward, the
    seventh wants to fall, and the whole chord aches toward the tonic a fifth below:
    V7 → I is the strongest resolution in Western music, the cadence the entire
    common-practice era is built on. The same chord, freed from its obligation, is the
    colour of blues and rock: a dominant seventh on every chord — I7, IV7, V7 — says
    "settlement deferred", which is one honest description of the blues. Jazz extended
    the serial: 9ths, 11ths and 13ths stack more thirds above the seventh, each addition
    a shade of ambiguity — and the more extensions a chord carries, the more places it
    can plausibly go, which is why jazz harmony feels simultaneously relaxed and
    unstable.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="drag the divider — one chord, two jobs" widget="compare" config={hcDominantCompare} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the key's chords are home, departure and tension, and the
    dominant seventh loads extra instability into the tension chord. Chained together, the
    functions become journeys: the progressions that popular styles all share.
  </p>

  <h2>Progressions: the journeys</h2>
  <p>
    Chain the functions and you get the repertoire's highways. <strong>I-IV-V-I</strong>
    is the folk and nursery skeleton (and the 12-bar blues's frame). <strong>ii-V-I</strong>
    is jazz's engine — the approach chord, the tension chord, the home chord — practised
    in all twelve keys until it is reflex. The <strong>Axis progression</strong>
    (I-V-vi-IV) underwrites "Let It Be", "No Woman No Cry" and hundreds more — it works
    because its tension pattern (home, tension, consolation, departure) is legible to
    any listener in any century. The <strong>12-bar blues</strong> is the classic worked
    structure: four bars of I, two of IV, back to I for two more, then one bar of V,
    one of IV, and a final I — twelve bars, three chords, with lyric lines shaped AAB
    over the frame (statement, statement again, punchline) and every instrument
    improvising on top of the form. Learn the skeleton once and you hear it in 1930s
    Delta recordings, Chuck Berry and most early rock: the progression is the room
    everyone is improvising in. Pop harmony's efficiency — often just four chords looping — is not a weakness:
    the craft moved into melody, arrangement and production, and the loop's familiarity
    is the canvas. Two working concepts finish the toolkit. <strong>Harmonic
    rhythm</strong> is how fast the chords change — one per bar feels calm, two per bar
    urgent, one per phrase monumental; the same four chords re-timed become a different
    song. And the <strong>circle of fifths</strong> is the progression map: neighbouring
    keys on the circle are the smooth modulations, V-I is one clockwise step, and whole
    repertoires wander its rim deliberately (jazz standards chain ii-Vs around it;
    Baroque sequences cascade down it).
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="pair each progression with the road it runs on" widget="match" config={hcProgressMatch} />

  <h2>Cadences: the punctuation</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>cadence</strong> is a chord pair that ends a phrase, and four types carry
    the meaning. The <strong>perfect cadence</strong> (V-I) is a full stop. The
    <strong>imperfect (half) cadence</strong> (ending on V) is a comma — a raised
    eyebrow demanding more. The <strong>plagal cadence</strong> (IV-I) is the "Amen" of
    hymns — gentler, warmer, less final than V-I. The <strong>interrupted (deceptive)
    cadence</strong> (V-vi) promises home and delivers somewhere else — the sigh of
    every song that refuses to end where you braced for it. Songs are structured by
    these gestures: verses breathe on imperfect cadences, choruses stamp perfect ones,
    bridges dodge. Film composers weaponise the last one: an unresolved cadence under
    a quiet shot does the audience's worrying for them. Train the ear with a two-minute
    game: play IV-I, then V-I, then V-vi back to back until the three endings feel
    different in the body — that felt difference <em>is</em> the theory, and everything
    else in this section is its vocabulary.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> dissonance is ugly and consonance is pretty. History
    argues otherwise: intervals the 9th century called unmusicable (thirds!) became
    core vocabulary by 1500; jazz treats 9ths, 11ths and 13ths — nominally "clashes" —
    as colours to linger on; and horror scores use pure, bare fifths to unsettle.
    Consonance and dissonance are roles in a drama, negotiated by style and context,
    not moral categories — the tritone exiled by medieval church music is the blues's
    favourite flavour and the structural pivot of the dominant seventh itself.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="true or false — the drama of dissonance" widget="judge" config={hcDissonanceJudge} />

  <h2>How the system was built</h2>
  <p>
    The functional harmony above is a historical invention with a traceable assembly
    line. Around the 9th century, singers began decorating chant in parallel — <em>organum</em>:
    the melody plus a fifth or fourth below, the first deliberate harmony. Parallel
    motion loosened into contrary motion, lines grew independent, and by 1200 Notre
    Dame polyphony stacked three and four voices by rule. What turned vertical sound
    into <em>chords</em> was a keyboard problem: accompanists needed shorthand, so
    <strong>figured bass</strong> (a bass note plus numbers for the intervals to play
    above it) made harmony a live skill by 1600 — the baroque "thoroughbass" is the
    ancestor of every guitar chord chart. Composers then noticed the patterns had
    <em>functions</em>: Rameau theorised the root and the triad (1722), and the
    I-IV-V logic was explicit by the classical era. Every device on this page — triads,
    sevenths, cadences, function — was not discovered but built, by working musicians
    solving practical problems, over about seven centuries.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="scrub seven centuries of assembly" widget="timeline" config={hcBuiltTimeline} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "functional harmony is a natural law that composers
  discovered." It is a historical construction with a traceable assembly line: organum,
  figured bass, Rameau's theory and the classical consolidation each added a layer to
  solve a working problem. Other musical cultures built different solutions to the same
  problem, which is exactly what the next section shows.</p>

  <h2>Pedals, drones and the refusal of harmony</h2>
  <p>
    One more device from the organ loft: the <strong>pedal point</strong> — a sustained
    bass note (usually the tonic or dominant) held while the harmony above changes.
    The ear hears two layers: the churning chords and the immovable floor beneath
    them; the floor builds expectation precisely because it refuses to join the
    argument. Classical composers open symphonies on tonic pedals (and Beethoven ends
    them there); rock and electronic music built genres on pedal tones and drones; and
    the drone itself is the deepest case: Indian classical music sustains the tanpura's
    reference pitch for an entire performance — a permanent pedal — and develops
    infinite melodic subtlety <em>without</em> chord change. Harmony is one powerful
    solution to the problem of organising simultaneous pitch; the drone is another,
    and the comparison is the cleanest evidence that "tension and resolution" can be
    melodic and rhythmic as much as harmonic. Other traditions agree on the refusal:
    Javanese gamelan layers shimmering inharmonic ensembles without functional chords;
    Chinese and Japanese classical musics build vertical colour from timbre, not
    triads; and much of the world's most powerful music has never needed a ii-V-I.
    Western functional harmony is one dialect — arguably the most automated — of a
    universal human activity: making simultaneous pitches mean something together.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="walk the decision — tension without chord change" widget="branch" config={hcDroneBranch} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Why is V7 to I the strongest resolution in Western music?</li>
    <li>Name the three primary chords of a major key and the function each carries.</li>
    <li>In the 12-bar blues, how many bars of I open the form, and what runs through the last four bars?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) The seventh adds a tritone, the most unstable interval
    in the system, so the chord aches inward and downward toward the tonic a fifth below.
    (2) I is home, IV is departure, V is tension demanding return; those three chords
    alone harmonise thousands of folk songs. (3) Four bars of I open it; the last four run
    V, then IV, then a final I, with lyric lines shaped statement, statement again,
    punchline.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Harmony is notes sounding together, organised into journeys: prediction management.</li>
    <li>Consonance comes from simple frequency ratios; dissonance is unstable energy, not bad sound.</li>
    <li>Stacking thirds makes triads; a major key supplies seven chords, with I, IV and V as the primary set.</li>
    <li>The dominant seventh's tritone makes V7 to I the strongest resolution in the system.</li>
    <li>Progressions are chains of functions: I-IV-V-I, ii-V-I, the Axis progression, the 12-bar blues.</li>
    <li>Cadences are harmonic punctuation: perfect, imperfect, plagal, interrupted.</li>
    <li>Functional harmony is one cultural solution; drones and gamelan organise the same expectations without chords.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The intermediate Music shelf continues naturally: "Form &amp; Structure" scales these
    chord functions up into whole pieces, "Rhythm &amp; Time Signatures" adds the time
    dimension this page left mostly still, and "Music History Eras" shows how the same
    harmonic machine changed costume across the centuries.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three layers for the curious. <strong>Voice leading</strong>: the craft beneath the
    labels — chords feel smooth when each singer's line moves minimally (stepwise,
    resolving the tritone inward, avoiding parallel octaves), which is why the same
    progression can be played as a block of stamps or a braid of lines; Bach chorales
    are the textbook because every voice sings a melody. <strong>Substitution</strong>:
    jazz's next lever — swap any dominant seventh with the one a tritone away (the two
    share the same unstable pair), turn ii-V-I into ii-♭II7-I, and suddenly the
    "same" song's harmony is redesigned in real time by performers; a jazz chart is a
    roadmap, not the territory. <strong>Psychoacoustics</strong>: modern theory grounds
    consonance in measurable roughness (Plomp and Levelt's curves, built from the
    ear's critical bands) — which explains why different cultures with different
    instruments land on different "consonances" (gamelan's inharmonic slendro octaves)
    while still organising music around tension and release. The deep summary: every
    musical culture manages expectation; the West industrialised it with functional
    chords, and everything this page described is that factory's floor plan.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Piston, Harmony; Huron, Sweet Anticipation (the psychology of expectation); Aldwell & Schachter; Plomp & Levelt (1965) on consonance. Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
</TopicPage>
